,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,34193,429,220,1124,58,18,20,59,50853,19,15,28,16,19,15,15,7110,60,9885,1045,7546,400,77,3,448,400,15,59,273,80,308,27,16,18,24,24,3,1741,429,400,77,3,374,279,12557,65266,27949,311,220,16,21,15,59864,220,16,18,24,24,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nNotice that  \\[35\\cdot40=1400.\\]Find some integer $n$ with $0\\leq n<1399$ such that $n$ is the multiplicative inverse to 160 modulo 1399.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"0e14dcac73cf2d5f2613b6327a479b2d836f845b6c37d4f9ab49942cce215068"},"seconds":60.249327421188354,"seed":383841768203554056,"stop":"eos","text":"To find an integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find an \\( n \\) satisfying the equation:\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399}. \\]\n\nThis means we need to solve the congruence:\n\\[ 160n - 1399k = 1 \\]\nfor integers \\( n \\) and \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399.\n\n1. **Euclidean Algorithm:**\n\\[\n1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 119\n\\]\n\\[\n160 = 1 \\cdot 119 + 41\n\\]\n\\[\n119 = 2 \\cdot 41 + 37\n\\]\n\\[\n41 = 1 \\cdot 37 + 4\n\\]\n\\[\n37 = 9 \\cdot 4 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n4 = 4 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\n\nThe gcd is the last non-zero remainder, which is 1. Since the gcd is 1, 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists.\n\n2. **Extended Euclidean Algorithm:**\nWe now backtrack to express 1 as a linear combination of 160 and 1399.\n\nStarting from:\n\\[\n1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot 4\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 4 = 41 - 1 \\cdot 37 \\):\n\\[\n1 = 37 - 9 \\cdot (41 - 1 \\cdot 37) = 37 - 9 \\cdot 41 + 9 \\cdot 37 = 10 \\cdot 37 - 9 \\cdot 41\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 37 = 119 - 2 \\cdot 41 \\):\n\\[\n1 = 10 \\cdot (119 - 2 \\cdot 41) - 9 \\cdot 41 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 20 \\cdot 41 - 9 \\cdot 41 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 41\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 41 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 119 \\):\n\\[\n1 = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot (160 - 1 \\cdot 119) = 10 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 160 + 29 \\cdot 119 = 39 \\cdot 119 - 29 \\cdot 160\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( 119 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\):\n\\[\n1 = 39 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) - 29 \\cdot 160 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 312 \\cdot 160 - 29 \\cdot 160 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 341 \\cdot 160\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n1 = 39 \\cdot 1399 - 341 \\cdot 160\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we see that:\n\\[\n-341 \\cdot 160 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is:\n\\[\nn \\equiv -341 \\pmod{1399}\n\\]\n\nTo find a positive \\( n \\) less than 1399:\n\\[\nn = 1399 - 341 = 1058\n\\]\n\nThus, the integer \\( n \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1058}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1058}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
